In article <21774@cfctech.cfc.com> kevin@cfctech.cfc.com (Kevin Darcy) writes:
>In article <YaAYAJC00XocQ21EQC@andrew.cmu.edu> aw1s+@andrew.cmu.edu (Andrew David Weiland) writes:
>>go through a rust trap twice.  The '/' command should identify traps (^)
>>that the player has found in PL 8.
>Strictly speaking, the '/' (whatis) command already does this, but *only* if
>the '^' is the current symbol for that square. Thus, arrow and dart traps may 
>be obscured. This is not really a problem, however, since if you a) know that 
>there is indeed a trap on that square, b) have a reasonable suspicion that 
>something shot out at you, and c) can use the whatis to find out *what* it 
>shot at you, logical deduction should take care of the rest. 

It's defeatist to say that since there is a way to do something, we're not
going to try to find a better way.  When I first started playing Nethack,
I was stuck by how lame the / command was.  In Moria (Yeah, I know, I'm
sure you guys get sick of being compared to Moria, but it's got a lot of nice
features), you can use / to identify a character, but you can also look
directly at something (traps included) and it will tell you what it is.  
I think this is something that definitely should be looked into, er,
no pun intended.

>>	As is, the repeat command is okay.  It relieves tedium, and stops
>>repeating commands when a character is hit.  But it doesn't stop when a
>>character starts feeling hungry, or if the character is
>>blind/hallucinating/confused when the blindness/hallucinations/confusion
>>wears off.
>>	I believe the repeat command should halt whenever the character's
>>condition changes (to hungry or weak, from confused or blind, etc), or
>>when a trap/secret door is found, in addition to when a character is
>>hit.
>
>program, also. Some people rest when they are confused, some don't. If you are 
>both stunned and hallucinating, which change-of-state should cause NetHack 
>to terminate your repeated rest command?

Again, look at the way Moria does things when resting.  If any state 
changes, it gives you a -More- prompt, allowing you to hit the
spacebar twice if you want to get out right away, or once if 
you want to continue resting.  A very smooth solution.

All in all, I like Nethack.  It has a lot of neat features, and much more
of a sense of humor than Moria.  But in terms of elegant solutions to
programming problems (such as those above, and movement through hallways)
Moria is still way ahead.  
 -Tom


